1. Modular IoT Sensor Gateway
Concept

A sensor hub application that collects data from multiple virtual or real sensors (e.g., temperature, humidity, motion), processes it locally, and sends it to a remote server or MQTT broker.

Why it’s great

Real-world embedded relevance.

Rich design-pattern use.

Demonstrates modular and template-based design.

Tech & Patterns

C++17: std::variant, std::optional, structured bindings, filesystem API.

Design Patterns:

Observer – sensors publish updates to subscribers (e.g., a logger or network client).

Strategy – different communication protocols (MQTT, HTTP, file log).

Factory Method – for creating sensor objects dynamically.

Environment: Embedded Linux (e.g., Raspberry Pi) or Ubuntu PC.

🧠 2. Multithreaded Job Scheduler / Task Executor
Concept

A lightweight task scheduling library that uses thread pools and template-based jobs for parallel processing.

Features

Submit any callable (lambda, function, functor).

Generic template-based task wrapping.

Task dependencies (e.g., via std::future).

Optional real-time constraints (for Embedded Linux).

Tech & Patterns

C++17: std::thread, std::async, std::future, if constexpr.

Design Patterns:

Command – each job encapsulates an action.

Singleton – global scheduler instance.

Builder – to configure scheduler properties (threads, affinity).

Environment: Ubuntu PC or Embedded Linux.


